Figure - The Phase 05 Todo API is intentionally in-memory. PostgreSQL and Prisma arrive later; this phase isolates HTTP semantics.

Step 1 - Generate the Todo feature
From: taskflow\apps\api

pnpm exec nest generate module todos
pnpm exec nest generate controller todos --no-spec
pnpm exec nest generate service todos --no-spec

Step 2 - Give the service temporary state
apps/api/src/todos/todos.service.ts - first read operations

FULL_STACK_DEVELOPER_2026_COMPLETE - Phase 05                                                         Build the In-Memory Todo API

import { Injectable, NotFoundException } from '@nestjs/common';

interface Todo {
id: number;
title: string;
completed: boolean;
}

@Injectable()
export class TodosService {
private nextId = 3;
private readonly todos: Todo[] = [
{ id: 1, title: 'Learn HTTP', completed: false },
{ id: 2, title: 'Build the API contract', completed: false },
];

findAll() {
return this.todos;
}

findOne(id: number) {
const todo = this.todos.find((item) => item.id === id);
if (!todo) throw new NotFoundException('Todo not found');
return todo;
}
}

The service owns temporary application state. The controller should not own an array just because it receives HTTP requests.
Later, Prisma replaces this in-memory persistence without changing the conceptual controller responsibility.

Step 3 - Add read routes
apps/api/src/todos/todos.controller.ts - read routes

@Controller('todos')
export class TodosController {
constructor(private readonly todosService: TodosService) {}

@Get()
findAll() {
return this.todosService.findAll();
}

@Get(':id')
findOne(@Param('id') id: string) {
return this.todosService.findOne(Number(id));
}
}

We convert the route parameter to a number for this protocol exercise. Formal validation and typed DTO boundaries are
deliberately deferred. The book should make that technical debt visible rather than pretend raw input is trustworthy.

Step 4 - Add creation
todos.service.ts - temporary creation behavior

create(title: string) {
const todo = {
id: this.nextId++,
title,
completed: false,
};
this.todos.push(todo);
return todo;
}

todos.controller.ts - POST /todos

FULL_STACK_DEVELOPER_2026_COMPLETE - Phase 05                                                                   Build the In-Memory Todo API

@Post()
create(@Body() body: { title?: string }) {
if (!body.title?.trim()) {
throw new BadRequestException('title is required');
}
return this.todosService.create(body.title.trim());
}

Nest defaults successful POST handlers to 201 Created. We use only a tiny manual guard here so the protocol example is
usable; this is not the validation architecture taught later.

Step 5 - Add partial update
todos.service.ts - patch behavior

update(id: number, changes: { title?: string; completed?: boolean }) {
const todo = this.findOne(id);
if (changes.title !== undefined) todo.title = changes.title;
if (changes.completed !== undefined) todo.completed = changes.completed;
return todo;
}

todos.controller.ts - PATCH /todos/:id

@Patch(':id')
update(
@Param('id') id: string,
@Body() body: { title?: string; completed?: boolean },
) {
return this.todosService.update(Number(id), body);
}

Step 6 - Add deletion with 204
todos.service.ts - removal behavior

remove(id: number) {
const todo = this.findOne(id);
const index = this.todos.indexOf(todo);
this.todos.splice(index, 1);
}

todos.controller.ts - DELETE /todos/:id

@Delete(':id')
@HttpCode(204)
remove(@Param('id') id: string) {
this.todosService.remove(Number(id));
}

A 204 response intentionally has no content. Returning a JSON success object would conflict with the meaning we are
teaching. Nest allows explicit status selection through @HttpCode(204).

Temporary limitation

The array disappears when the server stops. That is useful right now because the learner can focus on request/response
semantics. Do not add Prisma early to "fix" it. Persistence is a separate architectural problem and gets its own phase.

Official source notes
Primary protocol reference: RFC 9110 - HTTP Semantics (RFC Editor). Supplemental developer reference: MDN HTTP Reference and
HTTP Request Methods. Nest-specific routing and status behavior reference: NestJS Controllers documentation.

FULL_STACK_DEVELOPER_2026_COMPLETE - Phase 05                                                                       Build the In-Memory Todo API

This phase teaches HTTP semantics independent of a specific transport version. HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 change framing and transport details,
while request methods, target resources, headers, status codes and representation semantics remain the important application-level concepts
for this course.

